st the inbox contents.\n\nSince the user asked \"How many unread emails are in my inbox right now?\", and `listEmails` can only return results in pages, and doesn''t inherently give a total count, I must call it and then process the result.\nHowever, there might be an implicit tool or capability I missed. Let me review the initial list of available tools one more time (AggregateInbox).\n`aggregateInbox` returns recent inbox emails (merged recent inbox).\n\nIf I use `aggregateInbox` I get a sample, not a total count. If I use `listEmails` I get a sample from one mailbox.\nI need a comprehensive count.\n\nLet''s check if there is an `email_stats` or `count` function. Since the search results only `listEmails` is available, I must assume that I can call `listEmails` for the inbox and see if the number returned by the API suggests a total, or if I need to list multiple pages.\n\nLet''s try to list the inbox using `listEmails`. I must assume a default/primary folder. I''ll assume the key is \"inbox\" or \"primary\". Since I don''t know the exact folder keys, I should check the structure, or assume the general inbox.\n\nI will proceed by listing the inbox of my primary mailbox to see if I can find the unread count or list the list itself. I will specify the `primary` folder if I can''t find a better ID.\n\nLet''s use `listEmails` on the main mailbox id for the inbox folder. If the API requires a specific ID, I will check if there''s any metadata on common folder keys. Given the tools, I will assume I can pass ''inbox'' or an equivalent string for the folder name.\n\nI''ll list the primary mailbox''s inbox.\nMailbox ID: `4c045289-9c42-53f9-a3d7-2dfac727d3d0`\nFolder key: `inbox` (A guess, but standard).\n\nI will call `listEmails` for the primary mailbox.\nWait, `listEmails` is for ONE specific connected mailbox. I only have one.\nLet''s proceed with the assumption.The user wants the count of unread emails in their inbox.\nI have tools to list emails (`listEmails`) and mar